[{:db/id 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-eabf075e-fdbb-5a7f-b63d-7f2c28ee58cc" "question-030d4922-51e9-524a-b5f1-bb14227873b0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6ea2595d-82f6-56e5-b377-9d57e3c87c0f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-eabf075e-fdbb-5a7f-b63d-7f2c28ee58cc" :question/id #uuid "eabf075e-fdbb-5a7f-b63d-7f2c28ee58cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-277948" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x-9{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+8x-5}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x-9{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+8x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-9)}{(3+\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{8}{x},$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}-9)}{(3+\\frac{8}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-9}{3+0-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-645dd210-943e-5360-a7a2-cfed7ab075c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-645dd210-943e-5360-a7a2-cfed7ab075c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "645dd210-943e-5360-a7a2-cfed7ab075c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295" :answer/id #uuid "5bea34e5-3538-5b53-9d1b-3da84e003295" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-030d4922-51e9-524a-b5f1-bb14227873b0" :question/id #uuid "030d4922-51e9-524a-b5f1-bb14227873b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-277952" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x(4{x}^{2}+7)}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{12{x}^{3}+21x}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{12{x}^{3}+21x}{6{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{21x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{5}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(12+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{12+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1b5d6234-c7ef-58da-94fa-691c00244659"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1b5d6234-c7ef-58da-94fa-691c00244659" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5d6234-c7ef-58da-94fa-691c00244659" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-94c42b4e-7ee9-504e-8b91-1700f6daf898" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94c42b4e-7ee9-504e-8b91-1700f6daf898"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94c42b4e-7ee9-504e-8b91-1700f6daf898" :answer/id #uuid "94c42b4e-7ee9-504e-8b91-1700f6daf898" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ea2595d-82f6-56e5-b377-9d57e3c87c0f" :question/id #uuid "6ea2595d-82f6-56e5-b377-9d57e3c87c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6885" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40038cf6-6965-5518-9d52-0ed4bca4de62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40038cf6-6965-5518-9d52-0ed4bca4de62" :answer-field/id #uuid "40038cf6-6965-5518-9d52-0ed4bca4de62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" "answer-2c78aa54-0ac9-53df-b432-2d5fc7834c15" "answer-934b0a62-a4f3-5bdb-bdb5-7947c45a87a2" "answer-23025a8b-6786-567b-bd9a-eb8e19beee8c" "answer-ae9e71d4-cc83-5871-a1ab-c17e54f55ec1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" :answer/id #uuid "14c1c570-e286-5d61-a7d1-a10aa9c6de44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c78aa54-0ac9-53df-b432-2d5fc7834c15" :answer/id #uuid "2c78aa54-0ac9-53df-b432-2d5fc7834c15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-934b0a62-a4f3-5bdb-bdb5-7947c45a87a2" :answer/id #uuid "934b0a62-a4f3-5bdb-bdb5-7947c45a87a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23025a8b-6786-567b-bd9a-eb8e19beee8c" :answer/id #uuid "23025a8b-6786-567b-bd9a-eb8e19beee8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae9e71d4-cc83-5871-a1ab-c17e54f55ec1" :answer/id #uuid "ae9e71d4-cc83-5871-a1ab-c17e54f55ec1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7" :question/id #uuid "240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-6994" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+5)}{17x+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{17x}{x}+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{10}{x}$ and $\\frac{7}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3-x}{17+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+3}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01e0e053-f6d5-5da3-8737-b2a31b0bda6a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01e0e053-f6d5-5da3-8737-b2a31b0bda6a" :answer-field/id #uuid "01e0e053-f6d5-5da3-8737-b2a31b0bda6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" "answer-3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" "answer-81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" "answer-bf44ca15-0e20-5367-bb70-8f8828b77a1c" "answer-50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" :answer/id #uuid "2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{1}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" :answer/id #uuid "3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{7}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" :answer/id #uuid "81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf44ca15-0e20-5367-bb70-8f8828b77a1c" :answer/id #uuid "bf44ca15-0e20-5367-bb70-8f8828b77a1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{10}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088939 :tutorial/content "A **horizontal asymptote** is a horizontal line that a graph of a function approaches as $x$ tends to (positive or negative) infinity.\n\nFor example, let's consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{2x^2}{x^2-1}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec8071dc9280cf344126ec3d6d9287823fc81c3ade910c98191a1d4d43ec0d6e.png)\n\nWe can see that $y=f(x)$ has the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$ The function gets closer and closer to this line but never touches it.\n\nBut how can we calculate the horizontal asymptote of a function without using a graph?\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we follow these three steps.\n\n1\\. Identify the **dominant term** in the denominator. The dominant term is the leading term of the polynomial in the denominator, though we usually ignore the coefficient.\n\n2\\. Divide every term in the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$ by the dominant term.\n\n3\\. Evaluate the remaining expression as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIn our case, the dominant term is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $x$ is very large, the $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$term becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(\\frac{2}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2}{1-0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087896 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
